<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Goslar</h1><p class="st-meta">1290 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Goslar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Harz imperial city of the Salian emperors' palace and the Rammelsberg silver mine, whose wealth made it a leading Hanseatic town.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> was found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rammelsberg">Rammelsberg</a> near Goslar in the 10th century, and the Salian emperors built an imperial palace there, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Henry III</a> often resided. The town grew rich from mining and metal trade. After the decline of royal power it gradually took control of its own affairs and was a free imperial city by around 1290.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council dominated by mine owners and merchants ruled. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and lead from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rammelsberg">Rammelsberg</a> brought Goslar great wealth, and it joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. The city adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1528. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-riechenberg">Treaty of Riechenberg</a> of 1552 it lost control of the mine and forests to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-duke-of-brunswick-luneburg">Henry the Younger</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wolfenbuttel">Principality of Wolfenbüttel</a>, beginning a long decline.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Goslar lost its freedom in 1802 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-westphalia">Kingdom of Westphalia</a> from 1807 and to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hanover">Kingdom of Hanover</a> from 1816. Its old town and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rammelsberg">Rammelsberg</a> mine are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1290 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">imperial city</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.37%;width:10.19%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1290 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Goslar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Goslar</dd></dl></aside></div>
